The most important finding is that the builder appears to place too much reliance on a general master prompt to control a complex course-development process.
A master prompt can set rules and expectations. However, it is not a substitute for a structured development workflow, validation rules, subject-specific content controls and instructional-design checks.
The earlier output was substantial, but its length did not demonstrate that it met the intended learning requirements.
3. What would make eStudy genuinely different?
I would build around six differentiators.
A. A subject-specific curriculum engine
This should be the highest priority.
The builder must preserve the subject matter supplied by the course developer. It should not replace technical content with generic operational, compliance or procedural material simply because those structures are easier for an AI model to generate.
The system should distinguish between:
•	Course subject: What the programme is actually teaching.
•	Curriculum architecture: How the subject matter is organised.
•	Instructional design: How learners will learn the subject.
•	Assessment design: How learners demonstrate that they have achieved the outcomes.
•	Quality assurance: How the system checks that the course is fit for purpose.
For example, an AI for Chemical Engineering course should retain its technical focus on modelling, process optimisation, digital twins, predictive maintenance and process safety. Instructional design should make those topics easier to learn; it should never replace them with generic workplace procedures.
I recommend a mandatory curriculum-preservation rule:
The supplied curriculum, technical scope and subject-specific requirements are authoritative. Preserve all specified topics, concepts, skills and intended outcomes. You may improve sequencing, consolidate overlapping topics or recommend changes, but must not silently remove, replace or generalise subject content. Where requirements conflict, flag the conflict and request a decision before generating the full course.
Potential standout feature: a curriculum coverage checker that compares the original brief with the proposed course structure and shows exactly where each topic has been retained.
B. A proper instructional-design process
The builder should not move directly from a course description to a long generated manual.
I recommend a staged process based on backward design and constructive alignment.
Proposed eStudy development workflow
1.	Define the learning need
Identify the workplace problem, target learners, entry knowledge, performance gap and expected workplace application.
2.	Define measurable learning outcomes
Establish what learners should know, understand, demonstrate or perform by the end of the programme.
3.	Design the curriculum
Organise the subject matter into modules, units and lessons with a logical progression.
4.	Map assessment to outcomes
Decide how each outcome will be assessed, including knowledge, practical application and competency evidence.
5.	Generate learning materials and activities
Develop explanations, examples, case studies, scenarios, guided practice and learning resources.
6.	Validate and quality assure
Check subject accuracy, instructional alignment, assessment validity, accessibility, compliance and completeness.
7.	Prepare for delivery
Generate the learner and facilitator resources, LMS-ready content and implementation documentation.
This approach reflects the principle of constructive alignment: outcomes, assessments, learning materials and activities must work together.
The Quality Matters Higher Education Rubric, Seventh Edition, provides a useful reference. Its eight general standards cover course introduction, learning objectives, assessment, instructional materials, learning activities, technology, learner support, and accessibility and usability. It specifically emphasises alignment between these elements. 
Quality Matters+1
For eStudy's professional and workplace programmes, the Quality Matters Continuing and Professional Education rubric is also worth considering. It is designed for non-credit professional training and skills-based programmes. 
