C. A learning-outcome and assessment alignment engine
This could become one of the most valuable features.
Every course should have a traceable relationship between what learners are expected to achieve, what they are taught, what they practise and how their achievement is assessed.
For example:
Learning outcome	Learning activity	Assessment evidence
Explain the difference between mechanistic and data-driven models	Interactive explanation and worked example	Knowledge check
Evaluate the suitability of a regression model	Engineering case study	Model selection exercise
Interpret an anomaly detection result	Simulated process data scenario	Scenario-based decision
Recommend appropriate human verification controls	Process safety case study	Applied written response
The builder should automatically generate this mapping and check it.
It should flag:
•	Outcomes that are not taught.
•	Content that does not support an outcome.
•	Assessments that test knowledge not covered in the course.
•	Outcomes assessed only through recall when practical competence is expected.
•	Assessments that do not produce sufficient evidence of competence.
A particularly useful feature would be an alignment score with an explanation of the gaps. The score should be a quality indicator, not a claim of accreditation or certification.
D. More sophisticated assessment generation
Many AI course generators can create multiple-choice questions. That is not enough for professional development and competency-based learning.
The eStudy builder should support different forms of assessment according to the intended learning outcome.
