The system should also generate:
•	Model answers and explanations.
•	Assessment rubrics and marking criteria.
•	Feedback for correct and incorrect responses.
•	Clear pass requirements and weighting.
•	Assessment-to-outcome mapping.
•	Guidance on whether the assessment is formative or summative.
For competency-based programmes, it should distinguish between knowing how to do something and demonstrating that one can do it.
E. Source-based and technically validated content
This is particularly important for technical, regulatory, financial, health and safety-related courses.
The builder should not present plausible AI-generated statements as verified facts.
I would introduce a source management function that allows course developers to upload and identify:
•	Legislation and regulations.
•	Industry standards.
•	Technical manuals.
•	Organisational policies and procedures.
•	Research papers and textbooks.
•	Approved subject-matter expert material.
Every important technical or regulatory claim should be traceable to its source where appropriate.
The system should create a source register containing the document title, author or organisation, publication date, version, URL or document reference and the course sections it supports.
It should also distinguish between:
Source status
Verified
The statement is supported by an identified, reviewed source.
Source status
Needs review
The statement is plausible but needs confirmation by a subject-matter expert or authoritative source.
Source status
Unsupported
The system cannot identify adequate evidence. It must not present the claim as established fact.
For example, in the Chemical Engineering course, an AI-generated model recommendation should not be treated as technically sound merely because the explanation reads convincingly. Equations, units, assumptions, operating constraints and safety implications need appropriate verification.
F. South African relevance and accreditation readiness
This is an opportunity for eStudy to differentiate itself from international course-generation tools.
The builder could include a South African programme-design profile with configurable checks for:
•	NQF level and level descriptors, where applicable.
•	SAQA and relevant quality-assurance requirements.
•	QCTO requirements where the programme falls within that framework.
•	SETA requirements where relevant.
•	Professional body CPD requirements.
•	POPIA and information security considerations.
•	Relevant South African legislation and industry-specific regulatory requirements.
The important distinction is that the builder should support alignment and preparation for review, not automatically declare a programme accredited or compliant.
It should ask which framework applies to the programme rather than assume that every course needs an NQF level or QCTO alignment.
UNESCO's guidance on generative AI in education also provides a useful foundation for human-centred design, privacy, accountability and pedagogically appropriate use of AI. 
UNESCO+1
