I would also introduce three generation modes:
1.	Create from a brief: for new programmes where the developer provides the subject, learners, outcomes and requirements.
2.	Create from source material: for programmes based on existing policies, manuals, technical documents or learning material.
3.	Improve an existing course: for reviewing and upgrading an existing course without losing its approved content or structure.
This would make the platform useful throughout the course lifecycle, rather than only at the beginning.
6. A formal quality assurance framework
This is where I think eStudy could develop a real competitive advantage.
I would build a quality assurance engine into the platform, rather than relying on the developer to identify problems after the course has been generated.
The system should assess the course against a defined set of quality dimensions.
Proposed eStudy course quality framework
Illustrative scoring model — weights should be validated through pilot reviews.
Curriculum and subject coverage
20%
All specified topics and competencies are retained.
Learning outcomes and alignment
20%
Outcomes, content, activities and assessments are aligned.
Assessment quality
20%
Assessments validly measure the intended knowledge and skills.
Content accuracy and sources
15%
Technical accuracy, source traceability and evidence.
Learning experience
10%
Engagement, practice, feedback and logical progression.
Compliance and accessibility
10%
Relevant regulatory, privacy and accessibility checks.
Presentation and usability
5%
Consistency, readability, navigation and formatting.
Suggested release rule
A course should not pass simply because its average score is high. Critical failures in curriculum coverage, assessment validity, technical accuracy or mandatory compliance should prevent release until resolved.
I would use four possible QA outcomes:
•	Pass: Ready for final approval.
•	Pass with recommendations: No critical issues; minor improvements remain.
•	Revision required: Material instructional or content weaknesses.
•	Fail: Critical errors, missing curriculum content, invalid assessments or unresolved compliance risks.
A particularly important feature would be a review evidence report: the system should explain why it awarded a result and identify the specific course section that needs attention. It should never give a high score merely because the document is long or well formatted.
7. What should be improved in the generation process itself?
The earlier Chemical Engineering example points to a need for a different technical architecture.
I would not ask the AI to generate the entire course in one operation. I would split generation into separate, controlled tasks.
Generation stage	Output	Validation before next stage
1. Analyse brief	Structured course specification	Required fields and metadata
2. Analyse source material	Topic and evidence inventory	Source coverage and gaps
3. Design curriculum	Module and lesson structure	Original curriculum coverage
4. Develop outcomes	Course and module outcomes	Measurability and level
5. Design assessments	Assessment plan and mapping	Outcome coverage and weighting
6. Develop lessons	Lesson content and learning activities	Technical and instructional accuracy
7. Develop assessment items	Questions, tasks and rubrics	Correct answers, feedback and alignment
8. Conduct QA	Quality report and corrections	Critical checks passed
9. Produce deliverables	Manuals, guides and LMS outputs	Formatting, completeness and export validation
This would also help prevent one problem from affecting the entire course. If an assessment is weak, the assessment section can be regenerated without rewriting all the learning content.
I would introduce a human approval gate after stages 1, 3, 5 and 8. A subject-matter expert should approve the technical scope and content; an instructional designer should review the educational design; and an authorised person should approve compliance and release.
8. Recommended implementation roadmap
I would not attempt to introduce every feature at once. The first priority should be to make the existing generation process reliable.
Phase 1 · Immediate priorities
Make course generation reliable
•	Correct metadata mapping and locking.
•	Preserve the original curriculum.
•	Resolve conflicting course requirements before generation.
•	Generate and approve the course architecture first.
•	Improve learning outcomes and assessment alignment.
•	Introduce mandatory quality checks.
Success measure: the generated course accurately reflects the approved brief and does not omit or replace essential subject content.
Phase 2 · Development improvements
Build instructional-design intelligence
•	Add the outcome-to-assessment alignment engine.
•	Improve scenario and practical assessment generation.
•	Introduce source documents and reference tracking.
•	Add editable sections and selective regeneration.
•	Introduce assessment rubrics and feedback.
Success measure: instructional designers can produce a coherent, assessable course with less manual correction.
Phase 3 · Competitive differentiation
Develop the eStudy quality ecosystem
•	South African accreditation and compliance profiles.
•	Technical and regulatory validation workflows.
•	Collaborative review, approval and version control.
•	LMS export and integration.
•	Accessibility checks and learner analytics.
Success measure: eStudy can manage the quality and lifecycle of courses, not merely generate their initial content.
9. How I would test whether the improvements work
Before introducing a new version, I would establish a small benchmark set of five different course types:
1.	A technical course — AI for Chemical Engineering.
2.	A sales and customer service course — Sales Representative.
3.	A compliance course — financial sector consumer education.
4.	A professional learning programme — ICITP PLP.
5.	A practical workplace skills programme — one involving demonstrated workplace competence.
These are useful because they test different instructional requirements. A builder that can produce a reasonable generic course might still fail when it has to develop a technically demanding programme, a regulated course or a practical competency-based programme.
For every test course, compare:
•	Original brief versus generated course.
•	Curriculum coverage.
•	Accuracy of learning outcomes.
•	Assessment alignment and validity.
•	Quality and relevance of scenarios.
•	Technical and regulatory accuracy.
•	Repetition and unsupported statements.
•	Time required for instructional designer corrections.
•	Final quality assurance result.
I would also measure the percentage of generated content accepted without substantive correction. This is more meaningful than measuring the number of pages or words generated.
10. My overall recommendation
I see three possible product directions:
Direction	Product identity	My assessment
Basic generator	Turns a prompt into a course document	Too easy to replicate
Advanced AI authoring tool	Generates lessons, assessments and interactive content	Competitive, but crowded market
AI instructional design and course quality platform	Develops, validates, improves and prepares complete learning programmes for delivery	Strongest opportunity
The third option is where I would position eStudy.
The proposed differentiator could be:
eStudy Intelligent Course Development Platform
An AI-assisted course development system that combines subject-specific curriculum design, instructional design, competency-based assessment, technical validation and quality assurance to produce learning programmes that are relevant, measurable and ready for review and delivery.
It would be important not to market the system as automatically producing accredited courses. Rather, it should help course developers produce high-quality programmes that are better prepared for the relevant accreditation, professional and organisational review processes.
What I would prioritise first
If I were discussing this with the development team, I would put five requirements at the top of the agenda:
1.	Stop losing the subject matter. Curriculum preservation must be non-negotiable.
2.	Stop generating the entire course before its structure is approved. Introduce staged generation.
3.	Make assessment alignment automatic. Every learning outcome must have appropriate learning and assessment evidence.
4.	Build quality assurance into the application. The system must identify and explain weaknesses before the course is finalised.
5.	Make the output editable and traceable. Developers must be able to revise individual sections, track changes and see where content originated.
My view is that eStudy does not need to compete with Articulate on visual authoring or with every other platform on the number of AI features. It can compete through the quality of the instructional design, subject relevance, assessment integrity and South African professional learning context.
That is a more defensible position.
Suggested next step: formal product specification
I would recommend turning this evaluation into an eStudy Automatic Course Builder – Functional and Instructional Design Improvement Specification.
It could be used directly in a meeting with the developers and contain:
•	A gap analysis of the current builder.
•	A proposed revised user journey and application workflow.
•	Functional requirements for each development stage.
•	The revised master prompt architecture.
•	The curriculum preservation and alignment rules.
•	The QA framework with pass/fail criteria.
•	Five benchmark test cases.
•	A prioritised development backlog.
That would turn the recommendations into something the development team can work from, rather than leaving them as general suggestions.

